    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Several years ago, a young journalist asked me if I had any tips for him. At first, I wanted to say no. My career has been full of hard work, but I’ve also had some lucky moments. It’s hard to sum up my success in just a few tips. But in the end, I shared three golden rules that have guided me whenever I write "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "nonfiction",
            "zh": "非虚构作品"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Simple is smart. High school taught me to use big words. College teachers praised me for using big words. Then I graduated and realised that smart readers outside the classroom don’t want big words. They want big ideas made simple. Writers may think big words make them look smart. But too many big words can send a signal to some readers that the writer is showing off or doesn’t fully understand what he or she is saying. It actually takes a lot of work to express interesting ideas clearly in small words. The effort simply deserves more respect."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Be interesting. A good article should be fresh and important. Many stories are fresh, but not important. Sometimes great efforts at writing and reporting don’t attract an audience because the story fails to answer the silent question in every reader’s head: “Why should I care?” Other stories are important, but not fresh. If you have nothing new to say about a topic, don’t force it. Why would you want to write something that will be forgotten? Try to stand out!"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Write music. I’ve always wanted people to read my writing and remember it, and to talk about it. People naturally remember musical language, and I would encourage writers to add a little music to their stories. When you write, read your work aloud and feel the rhythm of the words in your voice. Here is a good example from John F. Kennedy: Ask not what your country can do for you; ask what you can do for your country. It easily sticks in people’s minds."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      }
    ]
  },
